From a868561d629869d88d1ed9c058e3bab8ad66b72e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Justin Pfister <119864532+JJRPF@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 07:21:38 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] rtc: macsmc: wake alarm on the PMU's alarm registers
 (T602x)

The RTC counter macsmc-rtc reads through the SMC (CLKM) lives in the
PMU, and the PMU has an alarm next to it that macOS
(AppleDialogSPMIPMURTC) uses for its dark wakes. Its registers are in
the ADT's spmi-mpmu node: alarm 0x140c, enable 0x1401 bit 6, event
0x14c0 bit 0 (write 1 to clear), interrupt mask 0x14c2 bit 0.

Checked on a J414s: the alarm register holds the counter's whole
seconds (CLKM >> 15; the value macOS left behind decodes to 22:30:58
of the evening it last slept), the event bit sets at that second, the
PMU raises SPMI peripheral interrupt 2, and the nub-spmi0 controller
drives AIC 724 with it once unmasked. The controller's reply (notify)
interrupt uses the same line.

- macsmc-rtc: optional alarm (read/set/irq_enable), one-shot like
  macOS's, from four NVMEM cells and an interrupt; a wakeup source
  with "wakeup-source".
- spmi-apple: IRQCHIP_SKIP_SET_WAKE. The parent is chained, and system
  suspend leaves chained interrupts enabled, so arming a peripheral
  interrupt for wakeup needs nothing from the hardware.
- t602x: nub_spmi0 becomes an interrupt controller on AIC 724, the
  PMU nvmem layout gains the four alarm cells, and the SMC RTC uses
  them with SPMI interrupt 2.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01AuzUDcEeRJQ6RCdWW2XWLb
